      "source": [
        "### Engineered Feature Results\n",
        "\n",
        "The engineered features show meaningful differences between customers who churned and customers who stayed.\n",
        "\n",
        "Customers who churned had a higher **AvgChargesPerTenure** value, with an average of about 62.68 compared to 57.78 for customers who did not churn. This suggests that customers who leave may be paying more relative to the amount of time they have been with the company.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **NewCustomer** feature showed a strong difference. About 55.5% of churned customers were new customers, compared to only 22.0% of non-churned customers. This supports the idea that customers with shorter tenure are at higher risk of churn.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **HighMonthlyCharges** feature also showed a noticeable difference. About 66.2% of churned customers had above-median monthly charges, compared to 44.1% of customers who stayed. This suggests that higher monthly costs may be connected to churn risk.\n",
        "\n",
        "The strongest engineered feature appears to be **MonthToMonthContract**. About 88.6% of churned customers were on month-to-month contracts, compared to about 43.0% of customers who did not churn. This confirms that contract type is one of the most important drivers of churn.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finally, churned customers had a slightly lower **ServiceCount** than non-churned customers, with an average of 3.13 services compared to 3.45. This may suggest that customers using more services are somewhat more engaged and less likely to leave.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the engineered features appear to capture useful churn patterns and should be included in the next model training step."
      ]
    },
    {
