    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "bx-qw8Z5sQ3x"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Logistic Regression With Engineered Features Results\n",
        "\n",
        "The logistic regression model with engineered features produced an accuracy score of 0.79 and a ROC-AUC score of approximately 0.837.\n",
        "\n",
        "The classification report shows that the model performs well at predicting customers who do not churn. For the non-churn class, the model achieved a precision of 0.84, recall of 0.89, and F1-score of 0.86. This means the model is strong at identifying customers who are likely to stay.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the churn class, the model achieved a precision of 0.63, recall of 0.53, and F1-score of 0.58. This means that when the model predicts churn, it is correct about 63% of the time. However, the recall of 0.53 shows that the model is only identifying about 53% of actual churned customers.\n",
        "\n",
        "The confusion matrix provides more detail. The model correctly predicted 917 non-churn customers and 198 churn customers. However, it incorrectly classified 116 non-churn customers as churn and missed 176 actual churn customers.\n",
        "\n",
        "From a business perspective, the false negatives are especially important because they represent customers who actually churned but were not flagged by the model. In a churn prediction problem, missing high-risk customers can be costly because the company loses the opportunity to intervene with retention offers or customer support.\n",
        "\n",
        "The ROC-AUC score of 0.837 suggests that the model has strong overall ability to separate churned customers from non-churned customers. However, the churn recall shows that there is still room for improvement. This supports the need to test more advanced models such as Random Forest and Gradient Boosting, which may better capture non-linear patterns in the data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4ZC4P3t3s9Yh"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 10. Random Forest Model\n",
        "\n",
        "After evaluating the logistic regression model with engineered features, the next step is to train a Random Forest model.\n",
        "\n",
        "Random Forest is an ensemble machine learning method that builds multiple decision trees and combines their predictions. This allows the model to capture non-linear relationships and interactions between features that logistic regression may not fully capture.\n",
        "\n",
        "Unlike logistic regression, Random Forest does not require feature scaling. The model can work directly with the encoded feature values.\n",
        "\n",
        "The purpose of this step is to determine whether a more advanced model improves churn prediction performance, especially recall for churned customers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pMk-3AgZtDhD"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Import Random Forest model\n",
        "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n",
        "\n",
        "# Create the Random Forest model\n",
        "rf_model = RandomForestClassifier(\n",
        "    n_estimators=200,\n",
        "    random_state=42,\n",
        "    class_weight=\"balanced\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Train the model using the feature engineered training data\n",
        "rf_model.fit(X_train_fe, y_train_fe)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Generate predictions\n",
        "y_pred_rf = rf_model.predict(X_test_fe)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Generate predicted churn probabilities\n",
        "y_prob_rf = rf_model.predict_proba(X_test_fe)[:, 1]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "rcf-fTantMwk"
      },
      "source": [
        "The Random Forest model is trained using the same feature engineered dataset used for the logistic regression model. The `class_weight=\"balanced\"` parameter is included because the churn class is smaller than the non-churn class. This helps the model pay more attention to churned customers during training."
      ]
    },
