    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "UtorODHkuhya"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Random Forest Results\n",
        "\n",
        "The Random Forest model produced an accuracy score of 0.78 and a ROC-AUC score of approximately 0.817.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the non-churn class, the model performed well, with precision of 0.83, recall of 0.89, and F1-score of 0.86. This shows that the model is strong at identifying customers who are likely to stay.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, the model performed weaker on the churn class. For churned customers, Random Forest achieved a precision of 0.61, recall of 0.48, and F1-score of 0.54. This means the model identified only 48% of actual churned customers.\n",
        "\n",
        "The confusion matrix shows that the model correctly predicted 920 non-churn customers and 179 churn customers. However, it incorrectly classified 113 non-churn customers as churn and missed 195 actual churn customers.\n",
        "\n",
        "Compared to the logistic regression model with engineered features, Random Forest had a lower ROC-AUC score and lower churn recall. This means that Random Forest did not improve the model’s ability to identify customers at risk of churn.\n",
        "\n",
        "The feature importance results show that the most important predictors were TotalCharges, tenure, AvgChargesPerTenure, MonthlyCharges, and MonthToMonthContract. This confirms that billing behavior, customer length of relationship, and contract type are major drivers of churn.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, Random Forest provided useful feature importance insights, but it did not outperform the logistic regression model. Because churn recall is important in this business problem, the next step is to test a Gradient Boosting model to see whether it can improve predictive performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "z758mMFDvrwJ"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 11. Gradient Boosting Model\n",
        "\n",
        "After testing Random Forest, the next advanced model is Gradient Boosting.\n",
        "\n",
        "Gradient Boosting is an ensemble machine learning method that builds decision trees sequentially. Each new tree attempts to correct the errors made by the previous trees. This allows the model to capture non-linear patterns and complex relationships in the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "The purpose of this step is to evaluate whether Gradient Boosting can improve churn prediction performance compared to both Logistic Regression and Random Forest."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AzEN8Twxvxqa"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Import Gradient Boosting model\n",
        "from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingClassifier\n",
        "\n",
        "# Create the Gradient Boosting model\n",
        "gb_model = GradientBoostingClassifier(\n",
        "    n_estimators=200,\n",
        "    learning_rate=0.05,\n",
        "    max_depth=3,\n",
        "    random_state=42\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Train the model using the feature engineered training data\n",
        "gb_model.fit(X_train_fe, y_train_fe)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Generate predictions\n",
        "y_pred_gb = gb_model.predict(X_test_fe)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Generate predicted churn probabilities\n",
        "y_prob_gb = gb_model.predict_proba(X_test_fe)[:, 1]"
      ]
    },
