    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "6d0Xw_NfyuP-"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Final Model Comparison Results\n",
        "\n",
        "The model comparison table shows that Gradient Boosting performed the best overall among the three models tested.\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic Regression with engineered features performed well as an interpretable baseline model, with an accuracy of 0.7925 and ROC-AUC of 0.8370. It also achieved a churn recall of 0.5294, meaning it correctly identified about 52.9% of customers who actually churned.\n",
        "\n",
        "Random Forest performed weaker than both Logistic Regression and Gradient Boosting. It had the lowest accuracy, churn recall, churn F1-score, and ROC-AUC. This suggests that Random Forest did not provide an advantage for this dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "Gradient Boosting produced the strongest results, with an accuracy of 0.7960 and ROC-AUC of 0.8404. It also had the highest churn precision, churn recall, and churn F1-score. Although the improvement over Logistic Regression was modest, Gradient Boosting provided the best overall balance of predictive performance and churn detection.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because the business goal is to identify customers at risk of leaving, churn recall and ROC-AUC are important metrics. Gradient Boosting performed best on both, so it is selected as the final model before hyperparameter tuning."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ueh7HDw8y2bR"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 13. Hyperparameter Tuning for Gradient Boosting\n",
        "\n",
        "After selecting Gradient Boosting as the strongest model, the next step is to tune its hyperparameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "Hyperparameter tuning tests different model settings to find a better combination of parameters. For Gradient Boosting, important hyperparameters include the number of estimators, learning rate, maximum tree depth, and minimum samples per leaf.\n",
        "\n",
        "The purpose of this step is to determine whether a tuned Gradient Boosting model can improve predictive performance compared to the initial Gradient Boosting model."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "laRO-IZfzEkL"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Import GridSearchCV for hyperparameter tuning\n",
        "from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Gv9ZlxX4zKQr"
      },
      "source": [
        "GridSearchCV is used to test multiple combinations of hyperparameters. It performs cross-validation on the training data and identifies the parameter combination with the best average performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "EPKy91LOzORI"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Define the parameter grid for Gradient Boosting\n",
        "\n",
        "param_grid_gb = {\n",
        "    \"n_estimators\": [100, 200],\n",
        "    \"learning_rate\": [0.05, 0.1],\n",
        "    \"max_depth\": [2, 3],\n",
        "    \"min_samples_leaf\": [1, 5]\n",
        "}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fppXQN9nzUyw"
      },
      "source": [
        "The parameter grid includes a focused set of values to keep tuning manageable while still testing meaningful model variations.\n",
        "\n",
        "The number of estimators controls how many boosting stages are used. The learning rate controls how much each tree contributes to the final model. The maximum depth controls how complex each tree can become. The minimum samples per leaf controls how many samples are required in each final leaf node, which can help reduce overfitting."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "MhtftfwNzaYC"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Create the base Gradient Boosting model\n",
        "\n",
        "gb_base = GradientBoostingClassifier(random_state=42)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7CNsDxiNzePH"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Set up GridSearchCV\n",
        "\n",
        "grid_search_gb = GridSearchCV(\n",
        "    estimator=gb_base,\n",
        "    param_grid=param_grid_gb,\n",
        "    scoring=\"roc_auc\",\n",
        "    cv=5,\n",
        "    n_jobs=-1,\n",
        "    verbose=1\n",
        ")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "a8Smf6McznNl"
      },
      "source": [
        "The tuning process uses ROC-AUC as the scoring metric because this project focuses on separating churned customers from non-churned customers. Five-fold cross-validation is used to evaluate each parameter combination more reliably."
      ]
    },
    {
