      "metadata": {
        "id": "SjF5_JLT3hXQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "The detailed SHAP summary plot shows both feature importance and direction of impact.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each point represents one customer. The color shows whether the feature value is high or low, and the position shows whether that feature pushed the prediction toward churn or away from churn.\n",
        "\n",
        "This helps identify not only which features matter most, but also how they influence churn risk."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qpNbhx4v3_VM"
      },
      "source": [
        "### SHAP Interpretation Results\n",
        "\n",
        "The SHAP results support the earlier Gradient Boosting feature importance findings. The most important feature was **MonthToMonthContract**, which had the highest average impact on the model’s churn predictions. This means that whether a customer is on a month-to-month contract strongly influences the model’s churn risk score.\n",
        "\n",
        "The second most important feature was **tenure**. This confirms the earlier business insight that customers with shorter tenure are more likely to churn, while customers who have stayed longer are generally less likely to leave.\n",
        "\n",
        "The feature **InternetService_Fiber optic** was also highly important. This suggests that customers with fiber optic internet service have a different churn pattern than other internet service groups. Based on the earlier exploratory analysis, this may be connected to higher monthly charges or customer expectations around service value.\n",
        "\n",
        "The feature **MonthlyCharges** ranked highly in the SHAP results, showing that customer billing amount is an important contributor to churn prediction. Customers with higher monthly charges may be more price sensitive or more likely to compare alternatives.\n",
        "\n",
        "The feature **PaymentMethod_Electronic check** was also one of the strongest predictors. This suggests that customers using electronic check payment may have a higher churn risk profile compared to customers using other payment methods.\n",
        "\n",
        "Other important features included **TotalCharges**, **Contract_Two year**, **AvgChargesPerTenure**, **TechSupport_Yes**, and **OnlineSecurity_Yes**. These results show that churn is influenced by a combination of contract structure, customer tenure, billing behavior, and service engagement.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the SHAP analysis improves model interpretability by showing which features have the largest impact on churn predictions. The results provide business insight that can help the company prioritize retention strategies around month-to-month customers, newer customers, high-charge customers, fiber optic customers, and customers using electronic check payment."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "RStvfQcF4Dsc"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 15. Final Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "This project developed a machine learning model to predict customer churn using customer account, service, contract, and billing data.\n",
        "\n",
        "The project began with data cleaning, exploratory data analysis, and baseline logistic regression modeling. The baseline model provided a useful starting point, but additional work was completed to improve model performance and interpretability.\n",
        "\n",
        "Feature engineering was then performed to create additional churn-related predictors, including AvgChargesPerTenure, NewCustomer, HighMonthlyCharges, MonthToMonthContract, and ServiceCount. These engineered features helped capture important business patterns related to customer behavior and churn risk.\n",
        "\n",
        "Several models were trained and compared, including Logistic Regression with engineered features, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and a tuned Gradient Boosting model. Gradient Boosting was selected as the final model because it achieved the strongest overall performance before tuning, with the highest ROC-AUC score and the best balance of accuracy, churn precision, churn recall, and churn F1-score.\n",
        "\n",
        "The original Gradient Boosting model achieved an accuracy of approximately 0.7960 and a ROC-AUC score of approximately 0.8404. The model also achieved a churn precision of approximately 0.6399, churn recall of approximately 0.5321, and churn F1-score of approximately 0.5810.\n",
        "\n",
        "Hyperparameter tuning was performed using GridSearchCV. Although the tuned model slightly improved accuracy and precision, it slightly reduced churn recall and ROC-AUC. Since the main business objective is to identify customers at risk of churn, the original Gradient Boosting model remained the preferred final model.\n",
        "\n",
        "SHAP analysis was used to interpret the final model. The most important churn drivers were MonthToMonthContract, tenure, InternetService_Fiber optic, MonthlyCharges, PaymentMethod_Electronic check, TotalCharges, Contract_Two year, AvgChargesPerTenure, TechSupport_Yes, and OnlineSecurity_Yes.\n",
        "\n",
        "From a business perspective, the results suggest that churn risk is highest among customers with flexible contract terms, shorter tenure, higher monthly charges, fiber optic internet service, and electronic check payment methods. These insights can help the company design targeted retention strategies, such as proactive outreach, contract conversion offers, service support, pricing review, or bundled service incentives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the project shows that machine learning can provide a useful churn prediction framework. The final Gradient Boosting model provides both predictive value and interpretable business insights that can support customer retention decision-making."
      ]
    },
