      "source": [
        "### Threshold Analysis Results\n",
        "\n",
        "The threshold analysis shows an important tradeoff between churn precision and churn recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "At the default threshold of 0.50, the Gradient Boosting model achieved the highest accuracy of approximately 0.7960 and the highest churn precision of approximately 0.6399. However, churn recall was only approximately 0.5321, meaning the model identified about 53.2% of customers who actually churned. The model also missed 175 actual churn customers.\n",
        "\n",
        "When the threshold was lowered to 0.35, churn recall increased to approximately 0.7193. This means the model identified about 71.9% of customers who actually churned. The number of false negatives decreased from 175 at the 0.50 threshold to 105 at the 0.35 threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "The tradeoff is that false positives increased from 112 to 216. This means more customers would be flagged as high risk even though they may not actually churn. However, in a churn prediction problem, this may be acceptable because the cost of contacting a customer with a retention offer is usually lower than the cost of losing a customer.\n",
        "\n",
        "The 0.35 threshold also produced the highest churn F1-score of approximately 0.6263 among the thresholds tested. This suggests that it provides the best balance between churn precision and churn recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "For this project, the original Gradient Boosting model remains the final model, but a 0.35 operating threshold is recommended for business use. The model can still return churn probability scores, while the 0.35 threshold can be used to classify customers as high risk for retention prioritization."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "rvN0ZK-o79bL"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 18. Final Model Selection Update\n",
        "\n",
        "The original Gradient Boosting model remains the selected final model because it achieved the strongest overall ROC-AUC score and the best balance of model performance before threshold adjustment.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, the threshold analysis showed that the default 0.50 classification threshold may not be the best business operating point. While the 0.50 threshold produced the highest accuracy, it missed more actual churn customers.\n",
        "\n",
        "For business use, a 0.35 threshold is recommended because it increased churn recall from approximately 0.5321 to 0.7193 and reduced false negatives from 175 to 105. This means the model would identify more customers who are likely to churn, giving the company a better opportunity to take retention action.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, the final recommendation is:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Use the original Gradient Boosting model as the final model.\n",
        "- Use ROC-AUC to evaluate the model’s overall ability to separate churned and retained customers.\n",
        "- Use a 0.35 threshold for retention targeting when the business goal is to prioritize churn detection.\n",
        "- Return churn probability scores in deployment so decision-makers can adjust the threshold based on campaign cost and retention capacity."
      ]
    },
